# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-runner-commit-message-breaks-powershell — a commit message with punctuation fails get_sources on the Windows runner
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Symptom

On the Windows runner, jobs fail in the built-in =get_sources= phase (before
any project script runs) with a cascade of PowerShell parser errors that quote
the *commit message* of the pipeline's HEAD commit. Seen on
=build:alfe:windows= (job 16466188546, MR !192 pipeline 2843757118):

: L'operateur << < >> est reserve a une utilisation future.
: Au caractere Ligne:282 : 134
: + ... m MR !43 (branch`ndocs-cador-3-host-interface-and-cador-spec, commit ...
: Argument manquant dans la liste de parametres.
: ...
: Jeton inattendu << normative >> dans l'expression ou l'instruction.
: ERROR: Job failed: exit status 1

The quoted text is verbatim from the cador-3 spec commit body (em dashes,
the section sign, backtick-adjacent tokens, apostrophes, =!43=/=!192=).

* Cause

GitLab Runner's PowerShell executor writes the job environment — including
=CI_COMMIT_MESSAGE= and =CI_COMMIT_DESCRIPTION= — into a generated =.ps1=
before =get_sources=. When the message contains characters that are special to
PowerShell (backtick =`= is its escape char; =$=, ="=, =<=, and the em dash /
section sign under the console codepage), an under-escaped assignment makes the
generated script fail to *parse*, so the whole job dies before cloning. It is
not the repo content or =.gitlab-ci.yml=; it is the runner turning a commit
message into code.

This is a known class fixed in newer GitLab Runner releases (proper here-string
/ single-quote escaping of the CI_* env assignments). The Linux/macOS shells
(bash) are unaffected because bash single-quoting is used.

* Impact

Low but recurring: it fires for *any* commit whose HEAD message carries such
punctuation, on the one platform not run locally. =build:alfe:windows= is
=allow_failure: true=, so it does not block a merge — but it reddens the job
and, on a release tag, wastes a Windows run. Every punctuation-heavy commit
hits it.

* Fix (pjb — runner-side; not fixable from the repo)

Upgrade GitLab Runner on the Windows box to a release that escapes the CI_*
environment assignments for the PowerShell shell (or set the runner's shell to
=pwsh= where the escaping is fixed). Confirm with a commit whose message
contains an em dash and a backtick.

* Workaround (in the repo's control, already adopted)

Keep commit *messages* plain ASCII — hyphens not em dashes, "section 11" not
"§11", straight quotes, no backticks, avoid the section sign — until the runner
is upgraded. The cador-3 notes commit (35d8cef5) was re-done in plain ASCII for
exactly this reason and its get_sources then parsed.

* Acceptance criteria

- A commit whose HEAD message contains =—=, =§=, and a backtick clones cleanly
  on the Windows runner (get_sources green).

* Note

Filed 2026-09-13 after pjb flagged the failing job. The failure is cosmetic to
merges (allow_failure) but is a real papercut and a foot-gun for release tags.
